## What Round Stone Size Goes in Which Setting?

This is the question every solitaire setting buyer needs to answer before adding a stone. Here are the right size ranges by setting.

**Tapered Grooved Solitaire (5–6mm band):** This setting reads best with a 0.10–0.25 ct round. It is band-first — the stone is an accent. A large stone breaks the proportion. A 0.15 ct round (3.3mm face-up) is the sweet spot. It is visible and intentional without dominating the band architecture.

**Ridged Solitaire (6–7mm band):** The ridged shank is designed to frame the stone. A 0.25–0.33 ct round (4.0–4.4mm face-up) fills the frame correctly. The ridged band visually amplifies the stone. A 0.25 ct reads larger than its carat weight because the band's parallel lines create visual depth toward the center.

**Structured Solitaire (6–7mm band):** The architectural shank is specifically built to amplify a round center stone. Use 0.25–0.50 ct. The structured shank makes a 0.33 ct round read like a 0.50 ct. This is the best value-per-visual-impact combination in the solitaire category.

**Channel Set (all widths — no additional stone needed):** Channel rings come with diamond weight already set. The Round Diamond Channel has 1/3 ct tw in the channel. The Hexagon and Brushed Finish have 3/8 ct tw. These are complete rings.

| Setting | Band Width | Ideal Stone Size | Face-Up Diameter | Stone Budget (lab certified) | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Tapered Grooved Solitaire | 5–6mm | 0.90–1.0 ct | 6.3–6.5mm | [0.90ct GIA — $2,487](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26429485?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | Stone accent; covers 90%+ of 7mm band |
